Arthur,<br />
<br />
My assessment memo on the Ridgeline claims is with your assistant in hard c=
opy; the short version is that the installation-defect theory is strong on =
the commissioning logs alone, and Ridgeline's spec-deficiency defense cuts =
against its own RFI record.<br />
<br />
The attached memo covers the separate point I flagged on Friday, and I want=
 to be sure it does not get lost in the Ridgeline work: Torvane appears to =
hold a substantial warranty claim against Brandt Thermal Systems on the reg=
enerative thermal oxidizer. The unit has never reached its guaranteed destr=
uction efficiency or its guaranteed gas consumption, and Brandt's own Novem=
ber 2010 service report concedes the shortfall. That claim is not against R=
idgeline and is not within the arbitration.<br />
<br />
The limitations analysis is in section 3 of the memo. The purchase order is=
 a mixed goods-and-services contract; on the seller-friendly reading the fo=
ur-year UCC period runs from tender of delivery in 2010, and on any reading=
 a Pennsylvania four-year contract period applies. Accrual is disputable, b=
ut a court could fix it as early as initial performance testing in the fall=
 of 2010. If the claim is to be preserved, the safe course is a tolling agr=
eement or suit well before the end of 2014, and we should not rely on the l=
ater accrual arguments.<br />
<br />
Recommendation: we put the claim and the limitations exposure to the client=
 in writing now, and ask for instructions - pursue, toll, or decline. Whate=
ver Torvane decides, the file should show the decision was the client's, ma=
de on our advice.<br />
<br />
Caleb<br />
<br />
Caleb Wisner<br />
Associate<br />
Prescott Vane LLP
